r the year after the year after  those languages aren't going anywhere  i'm under no pressure to learn those  languages i don't need them for my job  but it's just so much fun to contemplate  that i can go and learn these languages  and  you know discover  like i'm now discovering especially with  the persian because i've got these  wonderful courses on persian history  that sarah has has prepared for for link  which i enjoy going over repeatedly and  finally getting a sense of when the  sasanian dynasty was and when the  safavid dynasty was and the mongols and  the the turks and the uh  you know the various uh influence of  rome and and uh fighting with the  parthian empire i guess they called it  in english  um and so forth and so on so as a senior  you know here you are  uh  you could be saying well you know i've  i've lived most of my life uh you know  i'm kind of uh  i mean you don't want to sit there and  play solitaire uh you can discover new  things particularly now with covert  where it's not so easy to go traveling  around  you can travel through your language  learning  as a senior  and and they i don't think you know as a  senior certainly i don't say i can learn  this language and then that language but  i know that there are a lot of languages  there to learn and every language i have  learned has brought me so much  it's like being at a feast where there's  just this endless  sort of table with dishes and you can  eat as many as you want and just keep  eating and enjoying of course we know  that for seniors it's very good for  their brains so even if say my friend  who hasn't really been a language  learner like i've been a language  learner all his life  uh so maybe it's like the capacity to  learn languages is not as developed as  it is in my brain because i've been  doing it more  but  it's like if you haven't used the muscle  you have to slowly  start using it small steps small steps  gradually build up build up the strength  and i think the same 